    <mj-text
      padding="10px 12px"
      font-family="Austin News Text Web, TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif"
      color="#000000"
    >
      <h2 style="font-size: 24px; line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 400; margin: 0;">
        Financial fright
      </h2>
    </mj-text>
    <mj-image
          border-radius="10px"
          padding="10px 12px"
          width="600px"
          src="https://www.dubaisummary.com/email/images/REPLACE_ME.jpg"
          alt="REPLACE_ME"
          href="https://www.dubaisummary.com/"
          target="_blank"
        />

    <mj-text padding="10px 12px 16px 12px" font-family="Arial" color="#000000">
      <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>What’s happening:</strong> Dubai’s status as the Gulf’s leading financial centre is facing a serious stress test as the Iran conflict fuels security fears, market volatility, and rising unease among investors and expatriate professionals. Reports describe attacks and disruptions that have pushed banks, wealth managers, and major financial institutions into contingency mode. Although there has been no broad exodus of finance workers or client capital, anxiety has clearly risen, with firms across Dubai and Abu Dhabi confronting tougher questions about resilience, safety, and operational continuity.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>Wealth managers’ reactions:</strong> Even so, the response from the financial sector has been more cautious than panicked. Wealth managers in the DIFC, which hosts roughly 500 firms, have been fielding increased client questions about asset protection and backup plans rather than handling mass withdrawals. Major banks including Citigroup, Goldman Sachs, JP Morgan, Standard Chartered, and UBS shifted staff to remote work, while regulatory and administrative services moved online to keep key systems running. Clients appear to be taking a wait-and-see approach, helped by diversified holdings and booking centres in places such as Singapore and Hong Kong.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>What next:</strong> The bigger risk is not immediate collapse but slower erosion of confidence. Dubai’s economy depends heavily on trade, tourism, transport, real estate, and financial services, making it more exposed than oil-rich neighbours. Fundraising, dealmaking, and travel have already been affected, and markets have wobbled sharply. Still, analysts argue the UAE’s infrastructure, regulation, and long-term safe-haven appeal remain intact, at least for now.</p>
    </mj-text>
  </mj-column>
</mj-section>
<mj-spacer height="10px" />
